= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $E=\mathbb C/\Lambda$, and let $\Lambda'$ be the inverse image of the <cyclic subgroup> $G$ under the quotient map. Then $\Lambda\subset\Lambda'$ is an inclusion of <Euclidean lattices> with $\Lambda'/\Lambda\cong\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z$. The <Smith normal form> supplies a basis $u,v$ of $\Lambda'$ for which
$$
\Lambda=\mathbb Z(Nu)+\mathbb Zv.
$$
Choose the orientation of this basis so that $\operatorname{Im}(v/(Nu))>0$. Dividing the coordinate on $\mathbb C$ by $Nu$ gives an isomorphism of <complex tori>
$$
E\cong \mathbb C/(\mathbb Z+\mathbb Z\tau),\qquad \tau=\frac{v}{Nu}.
$$
Here $u$ maps to $1/N$, and its class generates $\Lambda'/\Lambda$. Thus the isomorphism carries $G$ to the required <cyclic subgroup>. **Every pair is represented: $\Phi$ is surjective.** This argument avoids assuming that an arbitrary integer lift of a primitive vector modulo $N$ is itself primitive.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Suppose $\tau_1=(a\tau_2+b)/(c\tau_2+d)$, with $ad-bc=1$, and put $\alpha=(c\tau_2+d)^{-1}$. The identities
$$
\alpha=a-c\tau_1,\qquad \alpha\tau_2=d\tau_1-b
$$
show that multiplication by $\alpha$ takes one <period lattice> isomorphically to the other: its basis matrix has determinant $ad-bc=1$. If $N\mid c$, the image of $1/N$ is congruent to $a/N$ modulo the target <period lattice>. Moreover $ad\equiv1\pmod N$, so $a$ is a unit modulo $N$. The image therefore generates the same <cyclic subgroup>.

Conversely, a <holomorphic> group isomorphism of <complex tori> lifts to $z\mapsto\alpha z$, with $\alpha\Lambda_{\tau_2}=\Lambda_{\tau_1}$. Indeed its lift fixing zero is additive: its failure to be additive is a continuous lattice-valued function, hence zero. An additive <holomorphic> map is complex linear. Write
$$
\alpha=a-c\tau_1,\qquad \alpha\tau_2=-b+d\tau_1.
$$
The corresponding integral basis matrix has determinant $1$: complex multiplication preserves the real orientation, and both bases $(1,\tau_j)$ are positively oriented. Solving gives $\tau_1=(a\tau_2+b)/(c\tau_2+d)$. Preservation of the <cyclic subgroup> means $\alpha/N\equiv u/N\pmod{\Lambda_{\tau_1}}$ for some unit $u$ modulo $N$. Comparing the two real basis coefficients gives $N\mid c$. Thus the matrix lies in the <Gamma 0 congruence subgroup>, and **the classification is**
$$
\boxed{\mathcal E\cong\Gamma_0(N)\backslash\mathbb H.}
$$
This is the noncompact <modular curve> classification; no cusp points have been added.

